%[text] d) (5 marks) Suppose we use a lead/lag controller $D(s) = \\frac{K(s+3)}{(s+5)}$  for plant $G(s) = \\frac{50}{s^2 +12s+25}$ in the closed loop control system:
%[text] ![](text:image:5021)
%[text]  Sketch the root locus for $K \> 0$ using matlab.
% You code goes here
s = tf('s');

% Define plant G(s)
G = 50/(s^2 + 12*s + 25);              % e.g., G = Kp / (s^2 + a*s + b);

K = 1; % For Open-loop transfer function, K = 1
% Define lead/lag controller D(s)
% Example: D(s) = K * (s+z1)/(s+p1) * (s+z2)/(s+p2);
D = K * (s+3)/(s+5);

L = D*G;              % open-loop transfer function

figure; %[output:995c2ee7]
rlocus(L);            % root locus of D(s)G(s) %[output:995c2ee7]
grid on; %[output:995c2ee7]

% Optional: overlay constant damping ratio lines
zeta = [0.2 0.4 0.6 0.8];
sgrid(zeta, []); %[output:995c2ee7]

% If, for any gain K, all closed-loop poles lie in the left half-plane, closed-loop system is stable. 
% If the locus crosses into the right half-plane for some K, those gains make the system unstable.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% Find the K value for which the system start to become unstable.
% Sample root locus data
[P, K_vec] = rlocus(L);   % P is poles, columns correspond to gains in K_vec

% Find the smallest K where any pole has positive real part
isUnstable = any(real(P) > 0, 1);   % 1 x N logical: true if any pole RHP at that K
idx = find(isUnstable, 1, 'first'); % first gain where system becomes unstable

K_crit = K_vec(idx)      % critical gain where instability starts %[output:6e2ee0e6]
P_crit = P(:, idx)       % corresponding closed-loop poles %[output:79006f06]

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


%[text] **Stability**
%[text] The closed-loop system is stable for all K \> 0 since the root locus never crosses into the RHP (`K_crit = Inf`).
%[text] **Achievable Damping Ratios**
%[text] The locus remains entirely on the real axis, so all closed-loop poles are real. Only ζ ≥ 1 (critically or overdamped) is achievable — no underdamped response is possible with this controller.
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_DEBBC93E] e) (5 marks)  Consider the system shown below  where:
%[text] $D(s)=\\frac{K(s+0.1)}{s}\\;\\mbox{ and } \\;G(s)=\\frac{s+1}{(s^4+15s^3+50s^2+189s+200)}$
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:0a45)
%[text] Use Matlab's symbolic computing to compute the closed loop transfer function of the system. Assign the computed closed loop transfer function the variable $\\texttt{G\\\_cl\\\_symQ1}$
% You code goes here
syms s K

% Define symbolic plant G(s)
G_sym = (s + 1) / (s^4 + 15*s^3 + 50*s^2 + 189*s + 200);

% Define symbolic controller D(s)
D_sym = K * (s + 0.1) / s;

% Open-loop transfer function L(s)
L_sym = D_sym * G_sym;

% Closed-loop transfer function G_cl(s) = L(s) / (1 + L(s))
G_cl_symQ1 = simplify( L_sym / (1 + L_sym) ) %[output:0d7bb9f6]
