%[text] *Your explanation goes here:*
%[text] Using the closed‑loop characteristic polynomial from 1(e), I evaluated the closed‑loop poles across the gain values sampled by MATLAB’s rlocus. For each gain, I checked whether any pole moved into the right‑half plane. The first gain at which a pole becomes RHP is the critical gain K\_crit. All poles remain in the left‑half plane for 0\<K\<K\_crit, and the system becomes unstable for K\>K\_crit. MATLAB shows this transition occurs at approximately K=354.4924, so the closed‑loop system is stable for 0\<K\<354.4924.
%[text] 
%[text] g) (5 marks) For the value of $K$ when the system is marginally stable (i.e. the closed loop system has pole(s) of multiplicity 1 on the $j\\omega$-axis and the other closed loop poles are in the open left half plane - from your answer to part 1(f) you know the value of $K$ where this occurs), calculate the value of the poles on the$j\\omega$-axis. Assign the values to the variable $\\texttt{jOmega\\\_crossingQ1}$ . 
% Your code goes here
% Part 1(g) – Poles on the imaginary axis at marginal stability

syms s K Omega real

% Plant and controller (symbolic)
G_sym = (s + 1) / (s^4 + 15*s^3 + 50*s^2 + 189*s + 200);
D_sym = K * (s + 0.1) / s;

% Characteristic equation: 1 + D(s)G(s) = 0
char_eq = 1 + D_sym * G_sym;

% Substitute s = j*Omega
char_jw = subs(char_eq, s, 1i*Omega);

% Separate real and imaginary parts
eq_real = simplify(real(char_jw));
eq_imag = simplify(imag(char_jw));

% Solve for Omega and K (real solutions only)
sol = solve([eq_real == 0, eq_imag == 0], [Omega, K], 'Real', true);

% Convert symbolic Omega to numeric values
Omega_vals = double(sol.Omega);

% Keep only positive real frequencies
Omega_vals = Omega_vals(Omega_vals > 0 & isfinite(Omega_vals));

% Construct the imaginary-axis poles ±j*omega
jOmega_crossingQ1 = 1i * [-Omega_vals; Omega_vals];

jOmega_crossingQ1 %[output:2bcd92be]
