%[text] **3. State Space Control and Closed Loop Bandwidth** (30 marks)
%[text] Assume that the plant is given by:
%[text] $G(s) = \\frac{s+1}{s^2(s+4)(s+6)}=\\frac{s+1}{s^4 + 10s^3 + 24s^2}$
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_CABEB808] a) (5 marks) Given G(s) what is the controller canonical (phase variable) state space representation of the plant G(s)?
clear
% Your code goes here
A = [ 0   1   0    0; %[output:group:0fe92654] %[output:329c366d]
      0   0   1    0; %[output:329c366d]
      0   0   0    1; %[output:329c366d]
      0   0  -24  -10 ] %[output:group:0fe92654] %[output:329c366d]

B = [0; 0; 0; 1] %[output:88f0dedd]

C = [1 1 0 0] %[output:9bfa20f4]

D = 0 %[output:511fe70b]
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_4616DFCE] b) (5 marks) You want to design state-variable feedback controller that has 15% overshoot and settling time of 2 seconds for the plant.
%[text] What are the desired locations of the 2 dominant closed loop poles of the system?
% Your code goes here
% Desired specs
PO = 0.15;      % 15% overshoot
Ts = 2;         % 2 s settling time (2% criterion)

% Solve for damping ratio zeta from PO formula
zeta = -log(PO) / sqrt(pi^2 + (log(PO))^2);

% Natural frequency from Ts ≈ 4/(zeta*wn)
wn = 4/(zeta*Ts);

% Desired dominant poles
sigma = -zeta*wn;
wd    = wn*sqrt(1 - zeta^2) %[output:3e009f76]
p1 = sigma + 1j*wd;
p2 = sigma - 1j*wd;

p1, p2 %[output:7c069e22] %[output:18d9712d]
%[text] **Where would you place the other two poles? Why.**
%[text] Place the remaining two poles significantly farther left on the real axis than the dominant pair, e.g.:
%[text] - s\_3=-10
%[text] - s\_4=-12 \
%[text] Reason:
%[text] Poles far to the left (at least 5 time farther left than the dominant pair) have much faster dynamics (large negative real part), so their modes decay quickly and do not significantly affect the transient response. This keeps the closed‑loop behavior effectively governed by the designed dominant pair at -2 + j\*3.32 and -2 - j\*3.32, preserving the desired 15% overshoot and 2 s settling time.
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_55259FDD] c) (5 marks) Use whatever matlab command(s) you want to confirm that your statespace realization from part [3(a)](internal:M_CABEB808) is controlllable and then compute a state feedback control matrix $\\mathbf{K}$ to place the poles at the desired locations from [3(b)](internal:M_4616DFCE) with the state feedback control $u(t)=-\\mathbf{K}x(t)$.
%[text] 
% Your code goes here
%% Choose remaining poles (fast, real, non-dominant)
p3 = -10; % hardcoded based on b's reasoning
p4 = -12; % hardcoded based on b's reasoning

desired_poles = [p1 p2 p3 p4];

%% Check controllability
Co = ctrb(A,B);
rank_Co = rank(Co) %[output:4cc4e01d]

if rank_Co == size(A,1) %[output:group:526fcda7]
    disp('System is controllable') %[output:12853dd0]
else
    disp('System is NOT controllable')
end %[output:group:526fcda7]

%% Compute state feedback gain K
K = place(A, B, desired_poles) %[output:89b19672]
%[text] 
%[text] d) (5 marks) Is the state space representation from part [3(a)](internal:M_CABEB808) above observable so that we can implement our state feedback controller given our current sensor outputs? Justify your answer.
% Your matlab code goes here.
%% Observability check

O = obsv(A, C);        % Observability matrix
rankO = rank(O);       % Rank of observability matrix
n = size(A,1);         % System order

disp('Observability matrix O = '); %[output:91ff4016]
disp(O); %[output:0f59a944]
disp(['rank(O) = ' num2str(rankO) ', n = ' num2str(n)]); %[output:24572f10]

if rankO == n %[output:group:25ad7735]
    disp('The realization IS observable.'); %[output:7f091c77]
else
    disp('The realization is NOT observable.');
end %[output:group:25ad7735]

% Uncomment the correct answer after checking above
RealizationIsObservable = true %[output:4a2d16ab]
% RealizationIsObservable = false
%[text] If the system is observable then design an observer output feedback matrix $\\mathbf{L}$ such that the observer system: $\\frac{d\\mathbf{\\hat{x}}}{dt}  =  \\mathbf{A}\\, \\mathbf{\\hat{x}}  + \\mathbf{B} \\, u  +  \\mathbf{L}(y - \\hat{y})$
%[text] $\\hat{y}  =  \\mathbf{C}\\, \\mathbf{\\hat{x}}$
%[text] has closed loop poles that cause the state estimation error to converge an order of magnitude faster than the desired closed loop plant dynamics (ie. the desired pole locations from (b) above)
% You matlab code goes here
% Design observer gain L using place function for desired pole locations
observer_poles = desired_poles * 10; % Place observer poles faster than system poles
                                     % 10 times faster than plant poles (this is fast enough)
L = place(A', C', observer_poles)' % Transpose for observer design %[output:3429c48a]
% % no valid full-order observer gain exists for this C and A

%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_695FA26F] e) (5 marks) Find the transfer function of the closed loop statespace system:
%[text] $\\begin{array}{rcl}\\frac{d\\mathbf{x}}{dt}(t)&=&(\\mathbf{A}-\\mathbf{BK})\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{B}r(t)\\\\y(t)&=&\\mathbf{Cx}(t)+\\mathbf{D}r(t)\\end{array}$
%[text]  you designed in part [3(c)](internal:M_55259FDD). Assign the transfer function to the variable $\\texttt{G\\\_clQ3}$.
% Your matlab code goes here.
%% Closed-loop A matrix
A_cl = A - B*K;

%% Closed-loop transfer function G_cl(s) = C (sI - A_cl)^(-1) B + D
sys_cl = ss(A_cl, B, C, D);
G_clQ3 = tf(sys_cl) %[output:91e7ef1b]
%[text] f) (5 marks) For the closed loop transfer function computed in part [3(e)](internal:M_695FA26F), find the bandwidth of the closed loop system using Matlab and assign it to the variable $\\texttt{omega\\\_BW}$:
%[text] 
% Your code here
% Compute closed-loop bandwidth (rad/s)
omega_BW = bandwidth(G_clQ3) %[output:42ba00cb]
%[text] 

